<TopicPage slug="subjects/radiography/foundational/x-ray-physics-and-safety">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Radiography is the discipline of making pictures with ionising radiation — safely. The physics
    has two halves. The first is <em>making</em> the radiation: a machine that hurls electrons
    into metal and harvests the X-rays that fly off. The second is <em>using</em> it: aiming a
    beam through a patient so that the differing absorption of tissues paints a shadow picture
    of the interior. Wrapped around both is an ethic unique to the job: unlike light or sound,
    X-rays carry a small but real capacity for harm, so every exposure must be justified — it
    must change or confirm the diagnosis enough to be worth the dose it adds. That ethic has a
    name, ALARA (As Low As Reasonably Achievable), and it is not a poster on the wall; it is the
    operating procedure of the whole profession, rebuilt into every exposure factor, every
    position and every room layout.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes the discipline learnable is that the physics is honest: the same handful of
    principles — how X-rays are made, how they are absorbed, how intensity falls with distance —
    explains the tube's dials, the image's greys, the department's lead aprons and the radiographer's
    standing position. This page walks those principles in order: production, exposure factors and
    image formation, then the safety framework that governs everything.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how X-rays are made, absorbed and justified" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />

  <h2>X-ray production: electrons, decelerated</h2>
  <p>
    An X-ray tube is a vacuum tube with two ends. At the <strong>cathode</strong>, a filament is
    heated until electrons boil off it (thermionic emission) and gather in a small cloud. Across
    a gap of a few centimetres, a potential difference of tens of thousands of volts — the
    <strong>kilovoltage peak, kVp</strong> — accelerates them at the <strong>anode</strong>, a
    spinning disc of tungsten alloy. When electrons slam into the target at half the speed of
    light, their sudden deceleration releases energy as X-ray photons, and it happens two ways.
    Most photons are <strong>bremsstrahlung</strong> ("braking radiation"): an electron skims past
    a tungsten nucleus, is deflected, and radiates off an amount of energy equal to however much
    speed it lost — which can be anything from almost nothing up to the full beam voltage. The
    result is a continuous spectrum from zero up to the kVp, the honest signature of random
    braking. A minority of photons are <strong>characteristic radiation</strong>: occasionally an
    electron knocks a tungsten K-shell electron out entirely, and an outer-shell electron falls
    into the vacancy, emitting a photon of a precise energy (tungsten's K-lines sit near 59–69
    keV). Characteristic lines matter clinically because they depend on the target material —
    which is why tungsten (high atomic number, high melting point) is the target of choice, and
    why molybdenum targets are used in mammography to shift the spectrum to softer, more
    tissue-contrast-sensitive energies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The beam that finally leaves the port is already a designed product: an aluminum filter
    absorbs the useless low-energy photons that would dose the skin without reaching the
    detector, and collimator blades trim the field to the anatomy needed. Two dials then shape
    every exposure: kVp sets the beam's <em>quality</em> — its penetrating power, and with it the
    subject contrast and scatter behaviour; the milliampere-seconds product (<strong>mAs</strong>,
    tube current times exposure time) sets the beam's <em>quantity</em> — how many photons, and
    with it, linearly, how much dose. Everything else in image production is the management of
    these two levers.
  </p>

  <h2>Exposure factors and the image: absorption is the picture</h2>
  <p>
    An X-ray image is a shadow map, and the shadows are made by <strong>differential
    absorption</strong>. As the beam crosses the body, photons are removed by interactions that
    depend on the tissue's density and effective atomic number: bone (calcium, high Z) absorbs
    far more than muscle and fat, which absorb more than air. What reaches the detector is
    therefore a pattern of intensity that encodes the anatomy's composition — bone leaves a pale
    shadow, air a dark one, and the greys of soft tissue between them carry most of the
    diagnostic work. At diagnostic energies the two absorption mechanisms matter differently:
    the <strong>photoelectric effect</strong> (photon fully absorbed, electron ejected) scales
    steeply with atomic number — approximately Z cubed — and produces the crisp bone–soft-tissue
    contrast; <strong>Compton scattering</strong> (photon deflected, energy shared) depends
    mostly on electron density and dominates at higher energies, producing the scatter that
    fogs the image from every direction. This is the physics behind the exposure-factor logic:
    raising kVp increases penetration and Compton scatter, reducing subject contrast and skin
    dose; lowering it boosts photoelectric contrast until penetration fails. Radiographers live
    on this trade-off — and on rules of thumb like the 15 per cent rule (raising kVp by 15 per
    cent approximately doubles receptor exposure, so kVp can be traded against mAs) and the
    inverse square law that governs source-to-image distance (SID): a standard chest radiograph
    at 180 cm rather than 100 cm needs the intensity of a much closer exposure and flatters the
    heart's size, while minimizing geometric blur.
  </p>
  <p>
    Managing scatter is half of practical radiography: a <strong>grid</strong> of thin lead
    strips between patient and detector absorbs the obliquely travelling scattered photons (at
    the cost of a higher mAs), and tight collimation reduces scatter at its source. Digital
    detectors then changed the economics of the greys: where film had a narrow tolerance and
    under- or over-exposed films had to be repeated, modern detectors respond over a huge range
    and the computer rescales any exposure into a viewable image. The hidden price is
    <strong>dose creep</strong> — because a digitally over-exposed image still looks fine, the
    feedback that once punished excess dose has vanished, and departments must police exposure
    indices deliberately. Automatic exposure control (radiation-measuring chambers behind the
    patient that cut the exposure at target receptor dose) exists to keep that discipline
    objective.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that radiation "stays in your body" after an X-ray, so the patient becomes radioactive or the doses "build up physically" like a poison. The photons pass through or are absorbed and gone in fractions of a second; nothing radioactive is implanted. What is genuinely cumulative is the <em>risk statistics</em>: each exposure adds a small increment of lifetime stochastic risk, recorded across a lifetime — which is why dose history matters, but why "how long until it wears off?" has no physical meaning.</p>

  <h2>Radiation safety and dose: ALARA in practice</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation biology separates two kinds of harm. <strong>Deterministic effects</strong> have
    thresholds — skin reddening around 2 Gy, cataract formation far higher — and radiography's
    doses sit nowhere near them; they belong to fluoroscopy accidents and radiotherapy errors.
    <strong>Stochastic effects</strong> — cancer induction chiefly — have no observed threshold
    and are modelled as probability rising with dose; they are the reason for the field's
    conservatism. The units track the distinction: <strong>gray</strong> (Gy) measures absorbed
    energy, <strong>sievert</strong> (Sv) weights it by biological effectiveness, and
    <em>effective dose</em> in mSv converts a given exposure into a comparable whole-body risk
    figure. The scale makes radiography's honest context: a standard PA chest radiograph
    delivers about 0.02 mSv — days of natural background; a CT abdomen, roughly 8–10 mSv;
    average natural background itself, around 2.4 mSv per year. The comparisons do not make
    dose trivial — they make <em>justification</em> and <em>optimisation</em> meaningful.
  </p>
  <p>
    ALARA's daily tools are the classic triad: <strong>time, distance, shielding</strong> — plus
    the engineering controls that work before anyone stands anywhere. Collimation and filtration
    cut the dose at its source; grids and tight beam restriction cut scatter; and the
    <strong>inverse square law</strong> governs the operator's own position — intensity falls
    with the square of distance, so doubling your distance from the source cuts your dose to a
    quarter. The calculator above makes it concrete: moving from 100 cm to 180 cm reduces
    intensity by a factor of 3.24; stepping back another step compounds it. During fluoroscopy,
    where the beam runs for minutes, the radiographer's habits — stepping back, standing behind
    the barrier, wearing the apron (0.25–0.5 mm lead equivalent), monitoring personal dosimeters
    — are the difference between a career dose and a regulatory problem. For patients, the same
    logic drives paediatric protocols (children are more radiosensitive and have more decades
    for stochastic effects to appear), pregnancy screening and the ten-day rule for
    abdominopelvic imaging, and the general reflex of asking, before every exposure: is this
    projection, this collimation, this technique the minimum that still answers the question?
    The profession exists because physics meets people — anxious patients who cannot hold still,
    injuries needing special projections, children — and because the judgement of technique
    against body habitus is human. Image quality, patient care and radiation safety are one job;
    which is why the registry certifies a person, not a machine operator.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="inverse square: intensity vs distance" widget="sliderLab" config={squareCfg} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The exposure arithmetic deserves one fully worked passage, because it is the daily
    reasoning of the profession. Suppose a knee radiograph is correctly exposed at 60 kVp and
    5 mAs, and the next patient is both larger and unable to hold still. The radiographer
    raises kVp by 15 per cent — 60 to 69 kVp — which approximately doubles receptor exposure,
    so the mAs can drop to 2.5 for the same detector signal: higher penetration for the
    thicker part, half the dose-quantity, and half the exposure time, which also halves the
    motion-blur risk in the uncooperative patient. Every element of the trade is now visible:
    kVp bought penetration and speed, mAs paid the dose bill, and the motion constraint chose
    where the saving landed. Reverse the case — a fine-detail extremity view on a thin,
    still patient — and the trade reverses: lower kVp for maximal subject contrast, more mAs
    accepted, because sharp grey-scale separation matters more than dose economy on a
    milligray examination. Technique charts simply store hundreds of such resolved trades;
    automatic exposure control generalises them by measuring the beam behind the patient and
    terminating exposure at the target receptor dose, converting judgement into
    instrumentation — with the radiographer still accountable for choosing the kVp that sets
    contrast, the grid that sets scatter cleanup, and the collimation that sets everything
    else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three refinements take you from technique charts to understanding. First, <strong>beam
    quality and quantity have their own vocabulary</strong>: the half-value layer (HVL) — the
    absorber thickness that halves the beam — is the practical measure of penetrating quality,
    and "beam hardening" describes how the spectrum itself shifts as filtration preferentially
    removes soft photons, a live consideration in CT artefacts. The anode heel effect — intensity
    varying along the anode–cathode axis because the target's own slant absorbs some exit beam —
    is exploited daily by orienting the cathode end over the thicker anatomy. Second,
    <strong>contrast media weaponise the photoelectric effect</strong>: iodine (K-edge ≈ 33 keV)
    and barium (≈ 37 keV) have absorption edges tuned almost exactly to the diagnostic spectrum,
    which is why a few milligrams per millilitre of them turn vessels and bowels luminous — a
    designed application of Z-cubed scaling. Third, the <strong>dose–risk debate is live</strong>:
    the linear no-threshold model underlying stochastic risk estimates is an extrapolation from
    atomic-bomb survivor data down to doses radiography actually uses, where epidemiology cannot
    resolve the risk; the profession's answer is not to argue the slope but to make the debate
    moot — collective dose discipline, justification frameworks (the ICRP's), and newer
    photon-counting detectors that keep dropping the dose per image. The physics of 1895 has been
    optimised for 130 years; the ethics of 1895 — the first radiographers burned their hands
    before anyone understood dose — is what turned it into a profession rather than a machine.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ARRT Radiography Content Specifications; Bushong, <em>Radiologic Science for Technologists</em>; ICRP dose recommendations.</p>
